  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM EST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Closed upper-level low pressure is \ncurrently centered near the Thumb with associated compact area of \nlow pressure making headway toward the eastern Lakes...trailing \nlarger/elongated parent low pressure over the eastern seaboard. Any \nlingering synoptic forcing/moisture continues to exit this \nafternoon, but still plenty cold for a lake response to continue. \nHeading into tonight, upper heights begin to rise and surface high \nbuilds in from the north and west, eventually bringing an end to any \nremaining lake effect showers. Attention then shifts to a wave \ncurrently making initial inroads into British Columbia. This wave is \nexpected to trek across Canada early this weekend, crossing Ontario \nduring the day Sunday. Attendant ~998mb sfc low expected to take a \nsimilar path. Some uncertainty as to just how far south impacts from \nthat are felt, with latest trends supporting a breezy and perhaps \noccasionally showery day (for some) on Sunday. Longer range \nensembles favor longwave troughing trying to set up across the \neastern half of NOAM mid-late next week. If this comes to fruition, \ncooler and occasionally unsettled would be the rule, with increasing \npotential for periods of rain and snow.\n\nForecast Details: Ongoing lake induced showers this afternoon likely \nto continue for a little while longer into this evening, but \ngradually diminishing in coverage as any lingering vestiges of \nsynoptic support continue to shift off to the southeast. Still \nfeasible that some of these showers may mix or change over to a bit \nof snow at times, again primarily across parts of the eastern U.P. \nand interior higher terrain of northern lower from near GLR to CAD. \nBy the overnight hours, diminishing showers should largely be \nconfined to primarily north flow lake effect areas of both far \nnorthwest and northeast lower, with a largely dry forecast in place \nby Saturday morning. Winds will be diminishing as well tonight with \nany lingering gustiness by Saturday morning limited to areas \ndirectly adjacent to Saginaw Bay/Lake Huron from Harrisville and \npoints south.\n\nPartly to mostly sunny skies return Saturday with near-normal \ntemperatures as highs largely span the 40s for much of northern MI. \nSome low 50s near Saginaw Bay. Attention then turns to Sunday as \naforementioned northern stream wave barrels through Ontario. \nnorthern MI should lie on the southern periphery of sfc low pressure \nwith a tightening pressure gradient renewing breezy to gusty winds. \nLower confidence on shower coverage, with latest ENS trends favoring \nthe highest PoPs across the eastern U.P., diminishing the farther \nyou head south.\n\nUpper troughing may prevail across the Great Lakes mid-late next \nweek. As was alluded to earlier, if this does in fact prevail, \nanother shot of cooler temperatures with occasional periods of \nrain/and or snow would be likely.",
